<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Ganghwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Japan–Korea Treaty of 1876</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Ganghwa was signed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in February 1876, after a Japanese naval clash near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a> in 1875. Modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a> imposed on Japan, it opened Busan and later two other ports to Japanese trade, gave Japanese residents extraterritorial rights and declared Korea independent, undermining Chinese suzerainty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ganghwa.jpg" alt="Treaty of Ganghwa" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan_Korea_Treaty_of_Amity_26_February_1876.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In January 1864 King Gojong came to the throne at the age of 12, and his father Yi Ha-ŭng, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Daewongun</a>, ruled in his name and set out to continue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s isolation. Western ships had appeared in Korean waters since the early 19th century, and in August 1866 the American merchant ship General Sherman steamed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong River</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>, where its crew fired on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> onshore, killing seven, and the ship was burned and its crew killed.</p><p>The French launched an unsuccessful expedition in 1866 and the Americans a victorious one in 1871, yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> stayed closed. After the Meiji restoration, an envoy arrived in 1869 with a letter using a character for the Japanese emperor that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> reserved for the Chinese emperor, and Korea, still within the Sinocentric order, refused to receive him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heungseon-daewongun">Daewongun</a> was later forced into retirement by Gojong and his wife, and officials who favoured trade took power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the morning of 20 September 1875 the Japanese gunboat Un'yō began surveying the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a>, where earlier clashes with foreign forces were fresh in memory. Commander Inoue sent a boat ashore to ask for water and provisions, and the Korean forts opened fire.</p><p>The Un'yō silenced their guns, a landing party burned several houses, and the Japanese, with modern rifles, routed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> who carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a>; thirty-five Korean soldiers were killed. The ship then attacked a fort on Yeongjong Island and withdrew. News reached the Japanese government on 28 September, and it sent warships to Pusan to protect Japanese residents. The treaty followed on 26 February 1876 and was negotiated chiefly by Kuroda Kiyotaka and Shin Heon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> used gunboat diplomacy to press <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> to sign what was an unequal treaty. Article 1 declared Korea an independent state with the same sovereign rights as Japan, which ended Joseon's status as a tributary state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. Article 2 provided for an exchange of envoys within fifteen months, and Article 4 ended the centuries-old role of Tsushima as intermediary.</p><p>Besides Pusan, Article 5 provided for two more ports to be found and opened in October 1877. Article 7 allowed Japanese mariners to survey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s coasts, Article 8 allowed Japanese merchants to reside and trade, and Article 10 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> extraterritoriality, the feature of earlier Western treaties most widely resented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It was the first of many unequal treaties Korea signed, and it left Korea open to the influence of imperial powers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Japan–Korea Treaty of 1876” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The regent Heungseon Daewongun, father of King Gojong, strengthened the throne from 1864 and repelled French and American expeditions, but Japan forced the country open with the <b>Treaty of Ganghwa</b> in 1876.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan forced Korea open with the unequal <b>Japan–Korea Treaty of 1876</b>, signed under the gunboat diplomacy of the Ganghwa Island incident.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
